docs/schwab-api-contract.md:30:| `Schwab::UnknownOutcome` | the same failures on a non-idempotent call (Phase 2 order POST); chosen by HTTP method so it can never be confused with the line above |
docs/schwab-api-contract.md:62:    "positions": [ { "longQuantity", "shortQuantity", "settledLongQuantity", "settledShortQuantity",
docs/schwab-api-contract.md:72:Cash-account `currentBalances`: `cashAvailableForTrading`, `cashAvailableForWithdrawal`, `cashCall`, `longNonMarginableMarketValue`, `totalCash`, `cashDebitCallValue`, `unsettledCash`, `accruedInterest`, `cashBalance`, `cashReceipts`, `longOptionMarketValue`, `liquidationValue`, `longMarketValue`, `moneyMarketFund`, `savings`, `shortMarketValue`, `pendingDeposits`, `mutualFundValue`, `bondValue`, `shortOptionMarketValue`.
docs/schwab-api-contract.md:74:Balance mapping: `equity` = `liquidationValue`; `cash_available_for_trading`; `unsettled_cash`; `cash_available_for_withdrawal`; `buying_power` nil for CASH. **`settled_cash` is derived, not a Schwab field.** Reading A: `cashAvailableForTrading - unsettledCash`. Reading B: `cashAvailableForWithdrawal`. Conservative: `min(A, B)` when both present. One named method, one spec per reading; Phase 2 guardrail 4 reads it.
docs/schwab-api-contract.md:125:`startDate`/`endDate` are epoch ms and override `period`. Valid combinations: day (1,2,3,4,5,10) with minute (1,5,10,15,30); month (1,2,3,6) with daily or weekly; year (1,2,3,5,10,15,20) with daily, weekly, monthly; ytd (1) with daily or weekly. Response `{"candles":[{"open","high","low","close","volume","datetime":<epoch ms>}],"symbol","empty","previousClose","previousCloseDate"}`. Daily `datetime` is midnight Eastern in UTC ms: convert in America/New_York. For the collar engine: `periodType=month&period=3&frequencyType=daily`.
docs/phase-2-plan.md:7:1. **Shadow mode stays on for the whole phase.** `SystemSettings#shadow_mode` is true by default in production; turning it off is a deliberate toggle on the system page with a typed confirmation, audited (`shadow_mode_toggled`, already a kind). In shadow mode the approval path runs end to end and the submission step writes a `would_have_submitted` audit event with the exact payload instead of calling Schwab. The number of shadowed approvals that must reconcile before it is turned off is the operator's call, shown on the system page as a count.
docs/phase-2-plan.md:8:2. **Bracket = Schwab `TRIGGER` strategy**: an entry order whose `childOrderStrategies` carries one `STOP` sell for the same quantity (`# SCHWAB_SHAPE_UNVERIFIED`: strategy type name, child shape, whether a `TRAILING_STOP` child is accepted; confirmed only by a shadowed payload compared against schwab-py's order builder and then by the first live submission). `docs/verify-on-approval.md` gets a Phase 2 section.
docs/phase-2-plan.md:10:4. **Idempotency is local (PRD §5.1):** `approved → submitted` runs under `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` on the proposal row, is the only code path that calls `place_order`, records a `submission_attempts` row before the call, and on timeout, 5xx, or a dropped connection after send moves the proposal to `submission_unknown`. `Submission::ReconcileJob` searches the orders endpoint for a match (instrument, side, quantity, order type, price, entered within the attempt window) before any retry; no retry without a reconcile that found nothing. A found order links back (`reconciled_order_id`) and the proposal becomes `submitted`.
docs/phase-2-plan.md:11:5. **Guardrails are data** in `risk_rules` (`rule_type`, `params`, `active`, `version`, `setup_id`, `superseded_by_id`): edits create a new version and supersede the old, never update in place. Evaluation results are stored on the proposal (`guardrail_results` jsonb: one entry per rule with `outcome` pass, warn, fail, or unavailable, plus the numbers) and audited (`proposal_evaluated`). Rules 2b (sector exposure) and 5 (earnings and econ blackout) evaluate to `unavailable` in Phase 2 because there is no sector or calendar source yet (FMP is Phase 2.5); `unavailable` is shown, never treated as pass.
docs/phase-2-plan.md:12:6. **Sizing is computed by Rails, never by the agent** (PRD §10): quantity = floor(risk budget ÷ (entry − stop)), risk budget = max-risk-per-trade % × equity from the latest balance; whole shares only (fractional support unverified). The agent proposes symbol, side, entry type and price, stop, thesis, setup; Rails fills the quantity and the collar.
docs/phase-2-plan.md:13:7. **Stop collar (§5.4.1)** computed from Schwab daily price history through `Agent::MarketData` (cached, budgeted): ATR(14) multiple, swing low over N bars minus a buffer, percent stop, chandelier (reference only). Parameters per setup in `risk_rules` rows of type `stop_collar`. Data-quality checks: at least 30 daily candles, latest candle within two trading days, a fresh quote. A stop outside the collar is a `warn` needing an override reason; rules 1 to 4 and 7 are hard `fail`.
docs/phase-2-plan.md:16:10. **Notifications in Phase 2 are email** (new proposal, fill or cancel, halt tripped, unprotected position, submission unknown), each a short mail with a deep link and no dollar figures in the subject; push arrives with the iOS shell (§12) and replaces them for the time-sensitive set.
docs/phase-2-plan.md:17:11. **iOS is the last step of the phase** and only its Rails side is in this repo: Hotwire Native path configuration, a device registration endpoint, and APNs delivery. APNs needs a gem outside Rails defaults (candidate `apnotic`); that is a decision for the morning, not this plan. The Xcode project is a separate repository.
docs/phase-2-plan.md:19:13. **Every state change on a proposal is an audit event** with the actor: `proposal_created`, `proposal_evaluated`, `proposal_approved` (with acknowledged warnings and any override reason), `proposal_rejected`, `proposal_submitted`, `would_have_submitted`, `submission_unknown`, `submission_reconciled`, `proposal_expired`, `proposal_cancelled`, `rule_changed` (before and after), `shadow_mode_toggled`, `unprotected_alert_sent`.
docs/phase-2-plan.md:25:| `order_proposals` (exists) | add `collar` jsonb (method values, bounds, where the stop sits), `sizing` jsonb (equity, risk budget, per-share risk, quantity), `override_reason` text, `evaluated_at`, `approved_at`, `approved_by_id`, `rejected_at`, `rejected_by_id`, `rejection_reason`, `submitted_at`, `expires_at`, `shadow` boolean (was submitted under shadow mode), `submitted_order_id` (the broker order the sync linked), `parent_proposal_id` (a stop adjustment points at the entry it adjusts) |
docs/phase-2-plan.md:26:| `submission_attempts` (exists) | as is; `outcome` enum submitted, would_have_submitted, unknown, failed |
docs/phase-2-plan.md:27:| `risk_rules` (exists) | add `mode` (block or warn, for wash sale), `note` text, `changed_by_id`; unique index on `(rule_type, setup_id)` where `active` with `NULLS NOT DISTINCT` |
docs/phase-2-plan.md:30:| `orders` | `order_proposal_id` (exists as lineage) is set by the submission and by reconciliation; `placed_via` becomes `app` for orders the app placed |
docs/phase-2-plan.md:34:`Guardrails::Evaluator.new(proposal).call` returns a `Guardrails::Report` (one `Result(rule, outcome, detail)` per active rule, `blocked?`, `warnings`, `needs_override?`) and is the only thing that writes `guardrail_results`. Rules are `Guardrails::Rule` subclasses keyed by `rule_type`, each `evaluate(proposal, context)` over a `Guardrails::Context` (equity and settled cash from the latest balance, open positions and their risk, today's realized P&L and the drawdown from snapshots, pending approved proposals projected onto cash, recent losses for wash sale, the collar): `max_risk_per_trade`, `max_positions`, `max_position_exposure`, `max_sector_exposure` (unavailable), `daily_loss_halt`, `max_drawdown_halt`, `settled_cash` (a buy must be fully covered by settled cash minus pending approved buys), `earnings_blackout` (unavailable), `wash_sale`, `notional_cap` (per order and per day, counting today's approved and submitted proposals), `stop_collar`. Evaluation runs on create, again on approve (a stale evaluation is refused: cash or positions changed), and on demand from the detail page. `evaluate_hypothetical_order` runs the same evaluator over an unsaved proposal and returns the report without writing anything.
docs/phase-2-plan.md:36:## 3. Stop collar
docs/phase-2-plan.md:38:`StopCollar::Calculator.new(instrument, entry_price:, setup:)` pulls one year of daily candles (`Agent::MarketData.price_history`, range `1y`) and the current quote, checks data quality, computes every enabled method from the setup's `stop_collar` rule params (`atr_k`, `swing_bars`, `swing_buffer_atr`, `percent`, `chandelier_bars`, `chandelier_k`), and returns `Collar(methods:, tight:, wide:, floor:, quality:)`. The percent stop is the floor: a stop below it is outside the collar whatever the other methods say. `StopCollar::Trail` recomputes chandelier and ATR trails for open positions nightly (`StopCollar::TrailJob`, 17:00 New York) and stores a `suggested_stop` per position for the EOD brief's "tighten stop" line; adjusting the real stop is a stop-adjustment proposal (kind `stop_adjustment`, target the working stop order, replace-only-tighter unless a human types a reason).
docs/phase-2-plan.md:42:`OrderProposal` states: `proposed` (created, evaluating), `validated` (no hard fail), `rejected_by_rules`, `approved`, `submitted`, `submission_unknown`, `filled`, `cancelled`, `rejected` (by the human), `expired`. Kinds: `entry` (buy with a child stop), `exit` (sell), `stop_adjustment`. Origin `agent` (through `propose_order`) or `human` (Flow 3 form). Transitions live on the model (`approve!(user:, acknowledged:, override_reason:)`, `reject!`, `submit!`, `expire!`), each audited, each refusing from the wrong state. Proposals expire at the end of the market day they were created on (`ProposalExpiryJob` via the tick after the close). The queue is `/proposals` (Flow 2 list), the detail `/proposals/:id` with the evaluation table, the collar with the stop marked, the sizing, the thesis and setup, the lots on a sell, the warnings to acknowledge, the override reason field, and the approve and reject forms (web: a typed confirmation word; iOS later: Face ID). The manual form is `/proposals/new` (Flow 3) with the collar and sizing recomputed on each change through a Turbo frame, no JavaScript beyond Turbo.
docs/phase-2-plan.md:46:`Submission::Submit.new(proposal, user:).call`: lock the row, refuse unless `approved` and `SystemSettings#writes_allowed?` for the real path (the manual halt blocks entries even in shadow mode; shadow mode itself is the gate between `would_have_submitted` and a real call), re-evaluate and refuse on a fresh hard fail, build `Schwab::OrderPayload.bracket(proposal)` (or `.sell`, `.replace_stop`), write the `submission_attempts` row with the payload, then either audit `would_have_submitted` (shadow) or call `place_order`. Outcomes: an order id → `submitted`, the attempt `submitted`, the next orders sync links the order and its child by `order_proposal_id`; a 4xx → `failed` on the attempt and the proposal back to `approved` with the error shown; a timeout, 5xx, or dropped connection after send → `submission_unknown` and `Submission::ReconcileJob` enqueued (retries at 1, 5, 15 minutes; then the operator sees "unknown, check Schwab" on the queue and the system page until resolved). `Orders::ProtectionWatch` runs in the orders sync: a parent filled with no live child stop, or a working stop gone at the broker, sends the unprotected alert email once per position and audits it.
docs/phase-2-plan.md:54:`Agent::Tool` gains `audited:`; `Agent::Tools.call` writes an `audit_events` row for an audited tool with the arguments (never the account hash). `Agent::Tools::ProposeOrder` (creates the proposal through the same service the form uses, returns the evaluation report and the proposal id; refused during a halt for entries), `AnnotateTrade` (post-mortem text on a trade, appended not replaced, audited), `AddWatchlistItem`, `EvaluateHypotheticalOrder` (read-only, no row). `Agent::Tools.for(:scheduled)` is unchanged by construction: the scheduled runtime never sees a write tool, and the existing eager-load check keeps it that way.
docs/phase-2-plan.md:62:`AlertMailer`: `new_proposal`, `fill_or_cancel`, `halt_tripped`, `unprotected_position`, `submission_unknown`; each sent once per event, audited, no figures in subjects, deep links only. The failure digest gains the submission attempts that failed.
docs/phase-2-plan.md:66:Proposal queue and detail (mobile-first), manual proposal form, rule editor, watchlist, the shadow-mode and halt controls on the system page with the shadowed-approval count, the dashboard's "pending proposals" line and the unprotected hard alert banner, the EOD brief's fills recap linking fills to proposals, and the journal's trade detail showing the originating proposal. Playwright scenarios for the queue with a warn, a hard fail, an override, an approved-in-shadow proposal, and a submission unknown.
docs/phase-2-plan.md:75:2. Stop collar calculator and the `stop_collar` rule; sizing; the nightly trail job and the EOD "tighten stop" line.
docs/phase-2-plan.md:76:3. `propose_order` through the registry with audited write tools; the manual form; the queue and detail with approve, reject, acknowledge, override; expiry.
docs/phase-2-plan.md:77:4. Submission in shadow mode: payload builder, the locked submit path, `submission_attempts`, `would_have_submitted`, `Schwab::Client#place_order/cancel_order/replace_order` with cassettes marked unverified, the reconcile job, orders linkage, the unprotected alert, the shadowed-approval count.
docs/phase-2-plan.md:81:8. iOS Rails side (path configuration, device registration, push delivery) once the APNs gem decision is made.
docs/phase-2-plan.md:86:Real submission with shadow mode off (the operator's toggle, after reconciled shadow approvals), options, FMP and the earnings and econ calendar (rules 2b and 5 stay `unavailable`), the allocation module, Phase 3 auto-submit, the Xcode project.
docs/verify-on-approval.md:21:10. `settled_cash` derivation: `cashAvailableForTrading - unsettledCash` (A) versus `cashAvailableForWithdrawal` (B), checked against the Schwab website on a day with unsettled proceeds. The app uses `min(A, B)`.
docs/artifacts/claude-code-kickoff.md:14:2. Schwab OAuth (three-legged) and the full token lifecycle from §5.1: 25-minute access refresh job, 7-day refresh-token expiry tracking, `submission_unknown` and reconciliation are Phase 2 but design the `orders` table for them now.
docs/artifacts/claude-code-kickoff.md:18:6. Dashboard with all four banner states (healthy / token expiring / token expired / halted). Halted can be reached via a manual flag in Phase 1 since no guardrails run yet.
docs/artifacts/claude-code-kickoff.md:24:Not in Phase 1: guardrail engine, proposals, approval UI, order submission, bracket stops, allocation module, FMP. Do not scaffold them beyond the migrations.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:17:- A guardrail engine (risk rules as data, not vibes) that evaluates every proposed order before it can reach Schwab.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:27:- Trading other linked accounts (read-only visibility into the IRA and any other accounts is in scope for wash-sale awareness; trading them is not).
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:49:**Why Rails and not just an agent:** the agent cannot be trusted with credentials, token refresh timing, idempotent order submission, or rule enforcement. Those are deterministic jobs. The agent is good at synthesis, briefs, screening, and post-mortems. Split accordingly.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:51:**Why an MCP server and not the agent hitting Schwab directly:** one credential holder, one audit log, one place guardrails run. The MCP tool surface is the contract.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:65:- **Order submission idempotency:** the Schwab order POST has no client-supplied order id (per community clients; confirm post-approval), so dedupe is local. The approved → submitted transition runs under a row lock and is the only code path that can call the order endpoint. On an ambiguous outcome (timeout, 5xx, connection drop after send), the proposal enters `submission_unknown`; a reconciliation job queries the orders endpoint for a matching order (instrument, qty, side, price, time window) before any retry. No blind retries, ever.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:73:- `order_proposals` (agent- or human-initiated; status: proposed → validated/rejected → approved → submitted → filled/cancelled; stores guardrail evaluation results as JSON; carries a submission_attempt record for the ambiguous-outcome path in 5.1)
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:79:- `audit_events` (append-only: approvals, rejections, collar overrides with reason, rule edits with before/after, kill-switch toggles, re-auths, shadow-mode toggles). Every mention of "logged" elsewhere in this doc means a row here.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:96:4. **Account-type specific (decided: cash account):** settled-cash check before any buy. Source of truth is the balances endpoint (settled cash / cash available for trading), not a local settlement ledger; the app only projects the effect of pending approved proposals on top of the broker figure. Rule is deliberately the conservative form: a buy must be fully covered by settled cash. (Buying with unsettled funds is legal if you don't sell before settlement; that path is intentionally not supported in v1 because it creates a second, time-dependent constraint on exits.) No shorting, no margin order types. PDT counter not needed.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:102:8. **Stop collar (see 5.4.1):** every entry proposal must include a stop, and the stop must fall within the computed collar or the proposal is flagged for override.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:104:Hard rule: guardrails are enforced in Rails. The agent can read them and reason about them; it cannot bypass or edit them through MCP.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:108:Purpose: replace gut-feel stop placement with a computed acceptable range ("collar") derived from several standard methods, evaluated against price history from Schwab Market Data (FMP fallback).
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:120:- Collar = [tightest candidate, widest candidate], with the percent stop acting as an absolute floor (a stop wider than the percent method is never inside the collar).
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:121:- Proposal evaluation returns each method's value, the collar bounds, where the proposed stop sits, and the resulting position size under the max-risk-per-trade rule for the chosen stop.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:122:- A stop outside the collar does not hard-block; it requires an explicit human override with a logged reason. (Guardrails 1-7 remain hard blocks where marked.)
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:135:- "Tighten stop" is a cancel/replace of the child order, which goes through the proposal pipeline as a stop-adjustment proposal (guardrails: new stop must be tighter than current for the Phase 3 auto-submit class; any loosening is human-only with a logged reason).
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:143:- `get_account_health` (equity, settled cash, drawdown, token status)
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:146:- `get_risk_rules`, `evaluate_hypothetical_order(params)` (dry-run guardrails)
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:149:- `propose_order(params, thesis, setup)` → creates order_proposal, runs guardrails, returns evaluation
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:164:One tool surface, two transports: the MCP server (remote, for the app) and the API tool definitions (in-process, for jobs) both delegate to the same service objects, so guardrails and read models cannot diverge between runtimes.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:176:- 07:00 ET pre-market brief: overnight moves on holdings/watchlist, gaps, today's earnings/econ events touching the book, stops at risk, settled-cash status. Delivered by email.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:186:- Proposal queue + proposal detail (the approve/reject decision; deliberate-confirm on approve; warn-acknowledge, collar-override-with-reason, and hard-fail states)
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:187:- Manual proposal form with live collar + auto-sizing feedback
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:209:- **Shadow mode (Schwab has no sandbox):** a global flag, on by default in production until deliberately turned off. In shadow mode the entire pipeline runs, including human approval, but the submission step writes a `would_have_submitted` audit event and the order payload instead of calling Schwab. Playwright runs in shadow mode. Phase 2 ships with shadow mode on; it is turned off only after a stated number of shadowed approvals reconcile cleanly against what would have been sent. Toggling it is an audit event.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:221:  1. Guardrail engine: exhaustive unit specs per rule, including the interaction cases (CSP-style cash reservation vs GFV check is Phase 3, but settled-cash math, wash-sale window, and stop-collar bounds are Phase 1-2). Table-driven specs so each rule's edge cases are enumerable.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:222:  2. Order proposal state machine: every legal and illegal transition (proposed → validated/rejected → approved → submitted → filled/cancelled), plus idempotency (double-submit of the same proposal must be impossible).
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:226:- **Playwright** for clickable UI: approval queue flow (approve/reject a proposal, override a stop outside the collar with a logged reason), dashboard renders with seeded data, risk-rule editor. These run against a seeded test DB with a fake Schwab adapter, not cassettes.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:232:A second rule set alongside the trading guardrails, covering the book-level behaviors from post-crash market research (concentration of best days, lump-sum vs staged deployment, threshold rebalancing, correlation breakdown, leveraged-proxy discipline). Kept structurally separate from per-trade guardrails: trading rules judge one order, allocation rules judge the whole book. Both feed the same proposal → approval pipeline; nothing here auto-executes.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:238:1. **Threshold rebalancing.** Nightly drift check against `allocation_targets`. When any weight drifts beyond its band (default 5 percentage points, per-target configurable), the agent generates rebalance proposals (sell overweight, buy underweight) through the normal pipeline with guardrail evaluation. The rule is mechanical; the approval is still human. A minimum trade notional (configurable, e.g., $100) suppresses churn on a small account; drift that would require a sub-minimum order is reported, not proposed. Fractional-share support in the order API is unverified; until confirmed, quantities round down to whole shares.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:239:2. **Leveraged-proxy exception.** Symbols tagged leveraged/amplified are excluded from mechanical rebalance proposals. Drift on those instead triggers a thesis-review task: the proposal renders the original thesis from the journal/watchlist next to current numbers, and the human decides deploy / hold / exit. Encodes "the discipline is the same, the decision is not mechanical."
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:252:**Phase 2 (propose + approve):** guardrail engine, order proposals via MCP, approval UI, bracket submission (entry + child stop, 5.4.2) from UI, ambiguous-outcome reconciliation, settlement checks from broker balances, wash-sale checks against the linked IRA, Hotwire Native iOS app with push and Face ID confirm (5.8a). Ships with shadow mode on.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:264:- Stop collar engine added as guardrail 8 / section 5.4.1.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:265:- IRA linked read-only for cross-account wash-sale checks.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:269:- Review v8 (Sep 1): real bracket stops at Schwab; local idempotency with reconcile-before-retry; unattended runtime is read-only; trades defined as zero-to-zero lifecycles keyed by CUSIP; shadow mode on by default; broker balances as settled-cash source; market calendar from Schwab; flow-adjusted equity series; min rebalance notional; single-writer memory repo; one append-only audit table.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:273:What "options in v1" would actually require, so the decision is informed:
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:277:**What a cash account can do (level-dependent):** long calls/puts, covered calls, cash-secured puts. No spreads without margin. Two of these create obligations the guardrail engine must model:
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:279:- **Cash-secured puts:** the engine must reserve settled cash equal to strike × 100 per contract and exclude it from "spendable" in every other proposal's GFV check.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:284:**Stop collar:** ATR/swing methods don't map to options premiums. Long options would use a premium-based max-loss rule (e.g., full premium is the risk, sized accordingly) instead of the collar.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-prd.md:298:- Order idempotency: no client order id on the Schwab side, so dedupe is local (row-locked single submission path, `submission_unknown` state, reconcile-before-retry; see 5.1). Duplicate submission is the one bug this system cannot afford.
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-wireframes.html:48:  .collar{margin:10px 0;}
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-wireframes.html:49:  .collar .track{position:relative; height:8px; background:var(--faint); border-radius:4px;}
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-wireframes.html:50:  .collar .band{position:absolute; left:25%; width:45%; top:0; bottom:0; background:var(--line); border-radius:4px;}
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-wireframes.html:51:  .collar .dot{position:absolute; top:-4px; width:2px; height:16px; background:var(--ink);}
docs/artifacts/investment-copilot-wireframes.html:52:  .collar .ends{display:flex; justify-content:space-between; font-size:10px; color:var(--mid); margin-top:3px;}
